 to show it  was support for palestinians it cost you  your tenure  it's a big claim what's the evidence for  it  well one is there's been a number of  candidates to come through who've been  denied tenure on to their deep  solidarity with the palestinian cause  alongside  their sophisticated scholarship one  includes in fact  a highly acclaimed jewish israeli sister  so it's not just a matter  of either black or white or or  palestinian or arab  is those who are concerned about truth  and condition of truth is to allow  suffering to speak and certainly the  suffering  among our precious palestinian brothers  and sisters are is just beyond  measure in terms of what is happening  under the israeli occupation  but in in beyond that though the issue  of how to come to terms with  israeli-palestinian  uh uh uh uh uh realities  uh it's difficult to have a respectful  conversation  anybody critical for the most part of  israeli occupations viewed as anti-jews  and anti-semitic  you get demonized overnight and so it's  very  clear that those who are willing to step  out  are going to be targeted in a variety of  different ways i'm not the only one i  mean mark lamont it was a whole  wave of folk who experienced this but  keep in mind this my brother  yes that i as an isolated person here at  home but you know still very privileged  and very  very very uh blessed that this kind of  devaluing and dismissing and  disrespecting of black people  people of color and those who are  willing to step  out and be critical of taboo issues in  this case the palestinian cause  that that's happening across the board  it's just not harvard this is community  colleges state colleges  universities including black colleges a  lot of people talk to west black  colleges and so forth so well  the black colleges are wonderful but  they've got their children  i'm not disputing of course that it's  very difficult about this subject and  that bad faith actors  the bad faith actors uh misuse uh the  anti-semitism charge in the context of  criticizing israel  but in terms of your own experience your  critics would say can you name someone  at harvard  who brought up the palestine issue with  you because your critics  like michael cohen who's a boston globe  columnist former state department of  speech  he did accuse you of anti-semitism he  says you shouldn't have said talking  about the israeli occupation of  palestine  is a taboo issue among certain circles  in high places which is what you said in  february he said you're feeding into an  anti-semitic trope by saying that  can you respond to him and can you offer  evidence of someone actually saying to  you you know what  cornell west you've got no future here  because of your pro-palestinian politics  well one anybody who wants to do serious  research  can go into the heart of the harvard  archive and come up with the rejection  of three high quality professors  all three of whom were deeply critical  of the israeli occupation  but would be in fact acceptable based on  academic  criteria in my case it couldn't be  academic criteria i was university  professor princeton  as well as harvard it could be a matter  of age but if that were the case then  ageism was operating and so i don't  think it's that  so it has to be political what other  candidates would it be in my case  my dear solidarity with bernie sanders  who i supported twice i don't think that  would be a strong enough issue that  would lead for them to be  so fearful of just allowing me to go  through a ten year process  after receiving tenure at yale in 1984  in university professors twice at  96 in 2002 what other reason  do i have it would be nice if the  harvard administration  harvest president were to lay out what  the argument is let's hear what it is  it's interesting that it's interesting  that harvard have not offered any real  substantive response to your accusations  so  we'll wait and see we'll we'll see what  happens there but stepping back for a  second there's someone in academia  can you put into context what's going on  on american campuses right now because  on the one hand  you hear from the right that school beds  are hotbeds schools are hotbeds for  liberal indoctrination  lefties are snowflakes liberals want to  cancel everything  and then you see people like nicole  hannah jones being denied tenure you see  the banning of critical race theory  uh you see what's going on with yourself  describe to us what's happening in  american universities right now  from your perspective but it's the  treatment of my discussion nicole hannah  jones is just  despicable and i'm glad to see she and  brother coats making their way to uh  to howard howard's a great great  institution and it's going to have its  own challenges in terms of  internal struggles unionization of his  adjunct professors the envy and  resentment that is true in any  professional context including including  universities but the problem is this my  brother  that there's a market  driven quality that every institution in  america so the united  the universities are have become so  commodified  they are bureaucratized and top heavy  so they're concerned about money  relation of donors and benefactors on  the one hand  and at the same time they've got  bureaucrats at the top that are obsessed  with diversity  but the shadow of jim crow the legacy of  white supremacy still cuts  very deep but in a very very subtle  manner  but a lot about diversity and equity and  inclusion and so forth and so on but  when you actually look at the structures  and the institutions of the place  even when it's more colorful at the top  with blacks and whites and asians and so  forth  translated on the ground and translated  on the ground means not just the impact  on students  but on the percentage of  different groups represented across the  board not all of them in afro-american  studies  all of them in a certain studies but  across the board you still see  shadows of jim crow so you get this  mixture and it's really  in some ways a lethal mixture of  commodification  market drivenness obsession with money  status  rankings and on the other hand the  inability of diversity to come to terms  of what is most  important which is the quest for truth  goodness beauty and some sense of the  whole if you're religious like myself  that's what university should be about  the counterweight against  free hatred hypocrisy  fear the freeing up of all of us to  engage in serious quests  so dr west let me let me jump in before  we run out of time in terms of the quest  for truth  we're seeing a battle against the big  lie in america we're seeing a battle  against voting rights from the  republicans  earlier today more than 150 companies  from pepsico to amazon to target  issued a letter voicing their support  for voting rights  um you have joe biden giving his big  speech yesterday  talking about democracy versus autocracy  have a listen  must ask those who represent us at the  federal state  local levels will you deny the will of  the people will you ignore their  voices we have to ask are you on the  side of truth or lies  fact or fiction justice or injustice  democracy or autocracy  that's what it's coming down to  do you agree dr west that is democracy  versus autocracy and if so  is joe biden up to the fight  well i hope he is if not we've got to be  as a group of citizens willing to engage  in collective action to preserve the  best of democratic projects  but no the united states is very  oligarchic at that point  it's hard to call us in any way a  substantive democracy the biden himself  has been so tied to corporate elites so  tied to wall street  so tied to the oligarchs in america now  when he what he calls  autocracy i would call authoritarian i  would call  escalating fascisms and nail fascism  both around the world as well as inside  the united states with trump and company  so that i think uh there's just  democratic aspects of oligarchic america  but we're in a much  much dimmer moment than than what he's  projecting and notice he just said mama  what about the filibuster

